---
title: Uniforms
description: Declare, pack, and update dynamic shader values.
---

Material uniforms define a stable WGSL buffer layout. Runtime calls update values inside that layout without recompiling the shader.

## Declaration forms

| Input value | Inferred type |
| --- | --- |
| Finite number | `f32` |
| 2-number tuple | `vec2f` |
| 3-number tuple | `vec3f` |
| 4-number tuple | `vec4f` |

Use `{ type, value }` for an explicit scalar or vector type and for `mat4x4f`. A matrix accepts a 16-number array or `Float32Array`; it is never inferred from array length.

```ts
uniforms: {
	uTime: 0,
	uColor: [1, 0.5, 0],
	uTransform: { type: 'mat4x4f', value: matrix }
}
```

Fields are available as `spektralUniforms.<name>`. Names must match `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`.

## Layout

Entries are sorted by name and packed using WGSL alignment. The final buffer is at least 16 bytes and rounded to a 16-byte boundary.

| Type | Alignment | Value size |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `f32` | 4 bytes | 4 bytes |
| `vec2f` | 8 bytes | 8 bytes |
| `vec3f` | 16 bytes | 12 bytes |
| `vec4f` | 16 bytes | 16 bytes |
| `mat4x4f` | 16 bytes | 64 bytes |

Sorting makes declaration order irrelevant to the resolved layout and signature.

## Runtime updates

```ts
useFrame((state) => {
	state.setUniform('uTime', state.time);
});
```

The name must exist and the value must match its declared type. Spektral merges runtime overrides with material defaults, compares the packed values, and writes only dirty byte ranges.

Changing a value does not rebuild the renderer. Adding, removing, renaming, or changing the type of a uniform changes the material signature.

## Frame uniforms

`spektralFrame.time`, `.delta`, and `.resolution` are generated separately and must not be declared in the material. See [Writing Shaders](/docs/writing-shaders#frame-uniforms) for their exact meaning.
